{"answer":"There are 7 subsets: {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}. For example, {1,8} has sum 9. Exhaustiveness follows by cardinality: the empty set sums to 0; a singleton cannot sum to 9 because every available element is at most 8; four distinct positive elements have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus only pairs and triples remain. The four pairs and three triples listed are the complete solutions in their respective cardinalities.","approaches":[{"description":"Partition all subsets by cardinality, rule out cardinalities 0, 1, and at least 4 using bounds, then enumerate the remaining pairs and triples.","id":"cardinality-split","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This gives a self-contained enumeration and a disjoint, exhaustive case split."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies the ground set and target sum.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-set-target","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The task concerns subsets of \"{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}\" whose elements \"sum 9.\"","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the cardinality bounds and complete pair/triple enumerations in the stated proof steps.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-set-target"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"total-count","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have element-sum 9.","step_ids":["empty-bound","singleton-bound","four-bound","pair-list","triple-list","combine-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The listed sums are verified in pair-list and triple-list.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["given-set-target"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["pair-list"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"empty-bound","justification":"The sum of no elements is 0, which is not 9.","statement":"A subset of cardinality 0 cannot be a solution."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"singleton-bound","justification":"Each member of the given set is at most 8, whereas the target is 9.","statement":"A subset of cardinality 1 cannot be a solution."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"four-bound","justification":"For four distinct positive integers, the least possible sum is 1+2+3+4=10. Adding further positive elements only increases the sum.","statement":"No subset of cardinality at least 4 can be a solution."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"pair-list","justification":"If a pair is {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, then a=1,2,3,4; the corresponding b values are 8,7,6,5. These are all in the given set, and no a>=5 can satisfy a<b and a+b=9.","statement":"The complete pair solutions are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"triple-list","justification":"Write a<b<c. If a=1, then b+c=8 and b<c gives (b,c)=(2,6) or (3,5). If a=2, then b+c=7 with b>2 and b<c gives (3,4). If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12, impossible. Hence the list is complete.","statement":"The complete triple solutions are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["empty-bound","singleton-bound","four-bound","pair-list","triple-list"],"id":"combine-count","justification":"Every subset has exactly one cardinality. The bounds leave only cardinalities 2 and 3; those disjoint cases contain 4 and 3 solutions respectively, so the total is 4+3=7.","statement":"The enumerated solutions are exhaustive and their total number is 7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
